@page "/exchart/surface"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="3D surface"
          Lead="A surface over a grid of two axes, as a volatility surface over deltas and tenors. Turn it, zoom it, read a point, and click a row of the grid to draw its slice beside it as a chart.">

    <DocsSection Title="A surface">
        <p>
            <code>ExSurface</code> takes a <code>SurfaceDefinition</code>: an axis across, an axis along, and a
            value at each point of their grid, or none. Drag to turn it, or use the arrow keys; <kbd>Shift</kbd>
            with a drag or an arrow pans, and the wheel or <kbd>+</kbd> and <kbd>-</kbd> zoom. Move over it to read
            the grid point nearest the pointer. Click a tenor or a delta to draw that slice beside the surface,
            a smile or a term structure, as an ordinary chart. A point with no value is drawn as a hole, never as
            a zero: the surface below has no quote at 25C 5Y. "Reset view" returns it to where it started, and
            the SVG and PNG buttons save the drawing as it stands, turned and zoomed as the user left it. The
            colour bar at the right says which value each colour is, and is saved with the drawing.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Surface)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/VolSurface.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Interpolated by the page">
        <p>
            As a chart's curve is, a surface between quotes is the application's. ExChart.Engine's Interpolation
            Helpers interpolate a grid of quotes, bilinearly here, into a function the surface samples, so the
            page chooses the method and the surface says it: under the surface and in the tooltip, with no dot
            at an interpolated value. The helper does not reach past the quotes, so a cell with a missing corner
            stays a hole.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <MudLink Href="showcase/fx-volatility" Target="_blank">FX volatility showcase</MudLink> marks the
            values it filled instead: its grid of quotes keeps the quoted values, a spline across each expiry's
            smile fills a quote that did not come in, and the surface is handed the grid with those values marked,
            so its tooltip says "quoted" or "interpolated" at each point.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SurfaceInterpolated)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceInterpolatedMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/VolSurface.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Coloured from a centre">
        <p>
            A P&amp;L, a Greek or a basis has a value that means nothing, usually zero. Coloured from its lowest
            value to its highest, a small loss beside a large gain would take the deepest colour, as alarming
            as a large loss. Set <code>Centre</code> on the <code>SurfaceDefinition</code> and the colours diverge
            from it instead: red below, blue above, each as deep as its distance from the centre, the same
            distance as deep on either side, and the band that holds the centre neutral. The colour bar then
            runs as far below the centre as above it, and always marks the centre. The colours are the Visual
            Tokens <code>--ex-chart-diverge-0</code> to <code>--ex-chart-diverge-8</code>, low to high, for a
            theme that wants green above.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SurfaceCentre)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceCentreMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/SurfaceShapes.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="On your own triangles">
        <p>
            Values on a triangle mesh, such as a finite element solution or quotes the application triangulated
            itself, are handed in with <code>SurfaceDefinition.FromTriangles</code>: the nodes, each a position and
            a value, and the triangles, each three node indices. The surface draws each triangle flat through its
            corners, which is the application's own piecewise-linear function, and never resamples it onto a grid.
            A dot marks each node, and the tooltip reads the nearest one. A node without a value, or a part of the
            domain with no triangles, is a hole. A click on a node slices through its position, along whichever
            axis the click lies nearer, and the slice cuts the triangles exactly. Between nodes its values are read
            linearly on the triangles, which the slice says. "View as table" lists the nodes. A triangle naming a
            node the mesh does not hold, a triangle with no area, or two nodes at one place is refused by name.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SurfaceTriangles)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceTrianglesMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/SurfaceShapes.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Logarithmic axes">
        <p>
            A grid's lines are evenly spaced by default, one per value, as a market quotes tenors and deltas.
            <code>SurfaceAxis</code>'s <code>Scale</code> places numbers in proportion to their value instead, or to
            their logarithm. The values must rise along the axis, and on a logarithmic axis lie above zero, or the
            surface is refused by name. <code>ZScale</code> puts the values on a logarithmic scale, with decade
            ticks on the walls. A value at or below zero keeps that scale proportional, with a Note saying why, as a
            chart's vertical axis does. The slice keeps both scales. Below, total variance over expiries spans three
            decades.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SurfaceLog)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceLogMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/SurfaceShapes.cs", "Examples/Chart/VolSurface.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="How it is drawn">
        <p>
            The surface is projected and drawn by the chart's own script while it is turned, because a round
            trip per frame cannot follow a drag. Blazor renders the toolbar, the tooltip, the slice and the table;
            the script reports the point under the pointer only when it moves onto another. "View as table" lists
            every value of the grid, or every node of a mesh.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
